<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pécs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sopianae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pécs is a city of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a> county, at the foot of the Mecsek hills. Founded as the Roman town of Sopianae, it has an early Christian necropolis now on the World Heritage list. In 1367 the first university in Hungary was founded there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pecs.jpg" alt="Pécs" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imoti95 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9cs_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pécs lies in the Carpathian Basin in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a>, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The Mecsek hills border it to the north and a rolling plain to the south, and the city has a significant mining past. The oldest archaeological finds in the area are about 6,000 years old, and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrived it was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>.</p><p>The Roman name Sopianae possibly comes from the plural of a Celtic word, sop, meaning marsh. The Hungarian name Pécs most likely derives from a Proto-Slavic or Illyrian word meaning five, echoing the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names Quinque Basilicae and Quinque Ecclesiae, which refer to five churches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded Sopianae at the beginning of the 2nd century, in an area peopled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and Pannoni tribes, as one of several wine-producing colonies in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. When Pannonia was divided into four administrative divisions, Sopianae became the capital of the division named Valeria. Charlemagne arrived in 791, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> ruled the area, and annexed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. A document written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> in 871 gives the first mention of the medieval city, as Quinque Basilicae.</p><p>Although the county's seat was the nearby castle of Baranyavár, Pécs became an important religious centre. Stephen I founded the Roman Catholic Diocese of Pécs in 1009, and Louis I the Great founded the first university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> there in 1367. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526 the armies of Suleiman occupied the city, and its centuries of Ottoman rule left the largest number of Turkish Ottoman buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, ordered by Joseph II in 1787, counted 8,853 residents. In 1777 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> had raised Pécs to the rank of free royal town, at a cost to the city of 83,315 forints. Industry grew in the second half of the 19th century, with iron and paper factories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and, after 1867, railways to Barcs and, from 1882, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pécs is the administrative and economic centre of Baranya County and the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Pécs. The Early Christian Necropolis of Pécs, Roman tombs and mausoleums from around the 4th century, was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2000. The city also keeps the Gazi Kasim Mosque, consecrated as a church after the Ottoman retreat, a cathedral of the 11th century and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s Palace of the 12th. It is known for the Zsolnay porcelain that decorates many of its buildings, and the University of Pécs, the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, remains based there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pécs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church, led by the archbishops of Esztergom and Kalocsa, owned great estates; universities were founded at <b>Pécs</b> in 1367 and at Pozsony in 1465, though neither lasted long, and the court of Matthias Corvinus brought Italian Renaissance art and humanism north of the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yugoslav forces occupied <b>Pécs</b> and the coal-mining region of Baranya in November 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-hungarian-baranya-baja-republic">Serbian-Hungarian Baranya-Baja Republic</a></li></ul></section>
